{
  "image_name": "0d356d1298deff95778909f892c51907.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ocean carbon sequestration pathways and how they move carbon from surface waters into the deep ocean and sediments. It contrasts four pumps: the solubility pump (SCP), the biological pump (BCP), the microbial carbon pump (MCP), and the carbonate counter pump (CCP).\n\nAt the surface, phytoplankton take up CO2 and produce organic matter. Part of this becomes sinking particulate organic carbon (POC), which drives the biological pump and can ultimately lead to organic carbon burial in seafloor sediment. The diagram also shows labile dissolved organic carbon being transformed by bacteria, archaea, and viruses into refractory dissolved organic carbon (RDOC), a long-lived form stored in deep water through the microbial carbon pump. RDOC also appears to interact with sinking particles through “coating” and aggregation effects, influencing burial.\n\nOn the right, coccolithophores produce carbonate, represented as sinking particles that transport carbon downward to the carbonate compensation depth (CCD). In sediments, microbial activity associated with alkalinity generation, such as denitrification and sulfate reduction, is linked to authigenic carbonate formation. Overall, the figure emphasizes that ocean carbon storage depends on coupled physical, biological, microbial, and carbonate processes acting across the euphotic, twilight, and deep-ocean zones.",
